Subject: [PATCH 28/28] pci.c wrote its property cells in HOST byte order
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.3(D) recorded two symptoms and named neither cause:

  * "QEMU,VGA@0 cannot be reached by path ... pnodename and pathres
    disagree about that node's unit address"
  * "No screen devalias on either arch. The FCode installs the node and
    its properties; nothing points at it"

The second is simply false -- /aliases has carried `screen` all along.
The first is true and is not about the VGA node: NO child of the PCI host
bridge could be reached by path, and the reason is four bytes in the
wrong order.

MEASURED. `ls` under the bridge listed five children and gave every one
of them the same unit address:

    pci8086,7000@0   pci8086,7010@0   pci8086,7113@0
    QEMU,VGA@0       e1000@0

yet their `reg` phys.hi cells are all distinct -- 80000, 90000, b0000,
100000, 180000. The first child's four bytes are

    B0=0 B1=8 B2=0 B3=0

which read LITTLE-endian is 0x00000800: bus 0, device 1, function 0, the
correct 1275 phys.hi. Read BIG-endian -- which is what decode-int does,
via `l@-be` -- it is 0x00080000, whose device field ((hi >> 11) & 0x1F)
is 0. So ob_pci_encode_unit() faithfully rendered "0" for all five, and
ob_pci_decode_unit("0") produced a phys.hi of 0 that matched none of
their reg cells. pnodename and pathres never disagreed; they agreed
perfectly about a value that had been byte-swapped underneath them.

THE REST OF THE TREE ALREADY KNOWS. libopenbios/bindings.c's
set_int_property() writes __cpu_to_be32(val) and get_int_property() reads
__be32_to_cpu(*p); every Forth reader goes through l@-be. Only pci.c's
builders handed set_property() raw host-order u32 arrays.

WHY IT SURVIVED THIS LONG: on a big-endian host the raw store is already
correct, and OpenBIOS's PCI support is exercised almost entirely on ppc
and sparc64. x86 and amd64 are the only little-endian targets. This is a
defect that exists only where nobody was looking, which is the whole
premise of this lab.

THE FIX wraps the cell stores in __cpu_to_be32, which is the IDENTITY
under CONFIG_BIG_ENDIAN -- so it is provably inert on ppc and sparc and
changes only the arches that were broken. Deliberately NOT wrapped, each
for a reason rather than by omission:

  * pci_set_AAPL_address()'s props[] is `cell`-typed, not u32, and the
    property is Apple/ppc-only; __cpu_to_be32 would truncate a 64-bit
    cell. It needs its own answer and has no little-endian caller to
    force one.
  * the literal props[] blocks under CONFIG_SPARC64 and CONFIG_PPC. They
    run only on big-endian hosts, where the raw store is already right.
    An untestable edit is not an improvement.

AFTER, on amd64 and x86 alike:

    14f470 pci8086,7000@1
    14fb60 pci8086,7010@1,1
    150028 pci8086,7113@1,3
    150788 QEMU,VGA@2
    151e58 e1000@3

    " /pci8086,1237@0/QEMU,VGA@2" find-dev   -> 150788
    " screen" find-dev                        -> 150788

which is QEMU's actual i440FX topology, function numbers and all. Both
bullets of 13.3(D) close on one fix.

WATCHED TO BITE. The `vga` track asserted `QEMU,VGA@0` -- the defect --
so it went red on the fix, which is the good-news failure a
characterization test exists to produce; it now asserts `@2` and names
both candidate causes in its message. Its `screen` line was an UNKNOWN
printed on every run and is now an assertion. Re-injecting the host-order
phys.hi fails the track by name.

One control bug of my own, caught the same way: find-dev returns
( phandle true | false ), so the probe's bare `.` printed the FLAG, and
`S=-1` read as "no phandle". The probe now says FOUND/NONE outright.

diff --git a/drivers/pci.c b/drivers/pci.c
--- a/drivers/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci.c
@@ -60,13 +60,41 @@
 #define IS_PREFETCHABLE		0x40000000
 #define IS_ALIASED		0x20000000
 
+/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.3(D): PROPERTY CELLS ARE BIG-ENDIAN.
+ *
+ * IEEE 1275 property-encoded integers are big-endian, and the rest of this
+ * tree knows it: libopenbios/bindings.c's set_int_property() writes
+ * __cpu_to_be32(val) and get_int_property() reads __be32_to_cpu(*p), and every
+ * Forth reader goes through `l@-be` (forth/device/property.fs). Only these
+ * builders handed set_property() raw HOST-ORDER u32 arrays.
+ *
+ * On a big-endian host that is the same thing, which is why the defect has
+ * hidden for as long as it has: OpenBIOS's PCI support is exercised almost
+ * entirely on ppc and sparc64. x86 and amd64 are the only little-endian
+ * targets, and there the bytes come out reversed.
+ *
+ * __cpu_to_be32 is the IDENTITY under CONFIG_BIG_ENDIAN, so every wrap below
+ * is provably inert on ppc and sparc and changes only the arches that were
+ * broken.
+ *
+ * DELIBERATELY NOT WRAPPED, and each for a reason rather than by omission:
+ *   - pci_set_AAPL_address()'s props[] is `cell`-typed, not u32, and the
+ *     property is Apple/ppc-only. A __cpu_to_be32 there would truncate a
+ *     64-bit cell; it needs its own answer, and it has no little-endian
+ *     caller to force one.
+ *   - the literal props[] blocks under CONFIG_SPARC64 and CONFIG_PPC
+ *     (bus-range fallbacks, virtual-dma, upa-portid, the interrupt-map
+ *     tables). They run only on big-endian hosts, where the raw store is
+ *     already correct. Wrapping them would be inert and untestable here, and
+ *     an untestable edit is not an improvement.
+ */
 static int encode_int32_cells(int num_cells, u32 *prop, ucell val)
 {
     int i = 0;
 
     /* hi ... lo */
     for (i=0; i < num_cells; ++i) {
-        prop[num_cells - i - 1] = val;
+        prop[num_cells - i - 1] = __cpu_to_be32(val);
         val >>= 16;
         val >>= 16;
     }
@@ -80,15 +108,15 @@
 
 	/* phys.hi */
 
-	phys[0] = flags | (space_code << 24) | dev | reg;
+	phys[0] = __cpu_to_be32(flags | (space_code << 24) | dev | reg);
 
 	/* phys.mid */
 
-	phys[1] = addr >> 32;
+	phys[1] = __cpu_to_be32((u32)(addr >> 32));
 
 	/* phys.lo */
 
-	phys[2] = addr;
+	phys[2] = __cpu_to_be32((u32)addr);
 
 	return 3;
 }
@@ -499,8 +527,8 @@
 	phandle_t dev = find_dev(config->path);
 	u32 props[2];
 
-	props[0] = config->secondary_bus;
-	props[1] = config->subordinate_bus;
+	props[0] = __cpu_to_be32(config->secondary_bus);
+	props[1] = __cpu_to_be32(config->subordinate_bus);
 
 	PCI_DPRINTF("setting bus range for %s PCI device, "
 	        "package handle " FMT_ucellx " "
@@ -877,8 +905,8 @@
 				     PCI_BASE_ADDR_0 + (i * sizeof(uint32_t)),
 				     config->assigned[i] & ~mask);
 
-		props[ncells++] = 0x00000000;
-		props[ncells++] = config->sizes[i];
+		props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(0x00000000);
+		props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(config->sizes[i]);
 	}
 	if (ncells)
 		set_property(dev, "assigned-addresses", (char *)props,
@@ -965,7 +993,7 @@
 
 		/* child address */
 
-		props[ncells++] = 0x00000000;
+		props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(0x00000000);
 
 		/* parent address */
 
@@ -977,7 +1005,7 @@
 
 		/* size */
 
-		props[ncells++] = config->sizes[i];
+		props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(config->sizes[i]);
   	}
 	set_property(dev, "ranges", (char *)props, ncells * sizeof(props[0]));
 }
@@ -1096,15 +1124,15 @@
         pci_decode_pci_addr(config->assigned[i],
                             &flags, &space_code, &mask);
 
-        props[ncells++] = PCI_BASE_ADDR_0 + (i * sizeof(uint32_t));
-        props[ncells++] = 0x0;
+        props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(PCI_BASE_ADDR_0 + (i * sizeof(uint32_t)));
+        props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(0x0);
 
         ncells += pci_encode_phys_addr(props + ncells,
                                        flags, space_code, config->dev,
                                        PCI_BASE_ADDR_0 + (i * sizeof(uint32_t)),
                                        config->assigned[i] & ~mask);
 
-        props[ncells++] = config->sizes[i];
+        props[ncells++] = __cpu_to_be32(config->sizes[i]);
 
         /* Store base of IO space for NVRAM */
         if (io_phys_base == 0x0 && space_code == IO_SPACE) {
@@ -2054,7 +2082,8 @@
                 val = (u32 *)reg;
 
                 for (i = 0; i < (len / sizeof(u32)); i += 5) {
-                    addr = val[i];
+                    /* the reg cells are big-endian; see encode_int32_cells */
+                    addr = __be32_to_cpu(val[i]);
 
                     /* Device address is in 1st 32-bit word of encoded PCI address for config space */
                     if ((addr & PCI_RANGE_TYPE_MASK) == PCI_RANGE_CONFIG) {
